# IPv4 Subnetting and Inter-LAN Routing Lab

## Overview

This Cisco Packet Tracer lab demonstrates IPv4 subnetting and communication between two separate LANs through a router.

The `192.168.10.0/24` network was divided into `/26` subnets. Four PCs communicate through two switches and one router.

## Network Topology

![Network Topology](network-topology.jpg)

### LAN 1

- PC0
- PC1
- Switch0
- Router0 interface G0/0

### LAN 2

- PC2
- PC3
- Switch1
- Router0 interface G0/1

## Devices Used

- 1 Cisco 1941 router: **Router0**
- 2 Cisco 2960 switches: **Switch0** and **Switch1**
- 4 PCs: **PC0, PC1, PC2, and PC3**
- Copper straight-through cables

## Addressing Table

| Device | Interface | IP address | Subnet mask | Default gateway |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| Router0 | G0/0 | 192.168.10.1 | 255.255.255.192 | N/A |
| Router0 | G0/1 | 192.168.10.65 | 255.255.255.192 | N/A |
| PC0 | FastEthernet0 | 192.168.10.10 | 255.255.255.192 | 192.168.10.1 |
| PC1 | FastEthernet0 | 192.168.10.20 | 255.255.255.192 | 192.168.10.1 |
| PC2 | FastEthernet0 | 192.168.10.70 | 255.255.255.192 | 192.168.10.65 |
| PC3 | FastEthernet0 | 192.168.10.80 | 255.255.255.192 | 192.168.10.65 |

## Subnet Information

### LAN 1: 192.168.10.0/26

- Network address: `192.168.10.0`
- Usable range: `192.168.10.1` to `192.168.10.62`
- Broadcast address: `192.168.10.63`
- Usable hosts: 62

### LAN 2: 192.168.10.64/26

- Network address: `192.168.10.64`
- Usable range: `192.168.10.65` to `192.168.10.126`
- Broadcast address: `192.168.10.127`
- Usable hosts: 62

## Physical Connections

| Source | Destination | Cable |
|---|---|---|
| PC0 | Switch0 | Copper straight-through |
| PC1 | Switch0 | Copper straight-through |
| Switch0 | Router0 G0/0 | Copper straight-through |
| Router0 G0/1 | Switch1 | Copper straight-through |
| Switch1 | PC2 | Copper straight-through |
| Switch1 | PC3 | Copper straight-through |

## Router Configuration

```text
enable
configure terminal

interface gigabitEthernet 0/0
ip address 192.168.10.1 255.255.255.192
no shutdown
exit

interface gigabitEthernet 0/1
ip address 192.168.10.65 255.255.255.192
no shutdown
exit

end
write memory
```

## Verification

### Router interface status

The following command was used on Router0:

```text
show ip interface brief
```

Both router interfaces showed `up/up`, confirming that the connections were working.

![Router Interface Status](router-interfaces.jpg)

### Inter-LAN connectivity test

PC0 belongs to the `192.168.10.0/26` subnet.

PC2 belongs to the `192.168.10.64/26` subnet.

The following command was run from PC0:

```text
ping 192.168.10.70
```

The successful ping confirms that PC0 can communicate with PC2 through Router0.

![Successful Ping from PC0 to PC2](successful-ping.jpg)

## Results

The lab was completed successfully:

- PC0 and PC1 can communicate within LAN 1.
- PC2 and PC3 can communicate within LAN 2.
- Router0 interfaces G0/0 and G0/1 are `up/up`.
- Devices in LAN 1 can communicate with devices in LAN 2.
- The `/26` subnet masks and default gateways work correctly.

## Skills Practiced

- IPv4 address assignment
- `/26` subnet calculation
- Default gateway configuration
- Router interface configuration
- Inter-LAN routing
- Connectivity testing with `ping`
- Interface verification using Cisco IOS commands
